From: ReadyNAS NG <readynasng@build>
Subject: [PATCH 23/27] padre_gmac: transmit checksum and scatter-gather by the engine, with a way back

Transmit checksum (TCP over IPv4) and scatter-gather through the GMT
engine, rebuilt from the 2.6.17 driver (GMAC description §5.3): a "meta"
descriptor (bit 26, 2 bytes {old command, new command}, no interrupt,
handed to the engine first), then one descriptor per buffer, the first of
the frame handed over last. The command bytes sit in ordinary memory, one
16-byte slot per transmit entry, like 2.6.17: read from the SRAM after
the rings, they are misread by the engine (the frame right after a command
gets the word before its checksum field, and commands no longer turn it
off), which was the failure of the first attempt; meta_ddr=0 brings that
back for comparison. Commands as the
2.6.17 driver sends them (read on the NAS with tools/gmpeek): 0xc0
(checksum 0x40 + TSO 0x80, frames never above the 1500-byte segment size)
for a frame left to the engine, 0x00 for any other frame, which must go
out with the engine off (it sums the L4 part again, field included); a
command only when the state changes. UDP (summed wrong by the engine), IP
options and fragments are summed in software.

On by default, with scatter-gather: padre_gmac.tx_csum=0 (or the same
parameter in /sys/module/padre_gmac/parameters at run time, or ethtool
-K eth0 tx off) turns it off, the sg parameter (ethtool -K eth0 sg)
scatter-gather alone. Tried on the NAS: loopback experiment as expected,
raw TCP, SSH and SMB transfers byte for byte, no checksum error on the
peer; reads +38 % (write) to +60 % (sendfile) over TCP, CPU no longer
saturated with sendfile. Still:

- every change of the feature restarts the MAC and the engine (2.6.17
  recovery: GMT mode 2, FIFO flush, open sequence), so that turning it
  off never relies on the engine obeying a command;
- the driver turns it off by itself, resets and logs, on a transmit
  timeout, 2 s without completion with frames pending, a burst of FIFO
  underruns, a descriptor still owned when it is reused, or TCP that only
  retransmits (padre_gmac.tx_guard, 4 retransmissions in 4 s making at
  least half of the segments; 0 = off);
- tx_csum_verify (echo 1 > /sys/module/padre_gmac/parameters/...) runs a
  scripted experiment in MAC loopback: test frames through the same
  transmit code, with and without commands, compared with a software
  checksum when they come back; it tells whether the engine sums, whether
  a reset clears it and how it takes a repeated command.

ethtool -S: tx_csum_hw, tx_csum_sw, tx_meta, tx_underrun,
tx_csum_fallback. With the feature off no command is ever sent and the
registers are written as before.
